      \field{abstract}{Graph neural networks (GNNs) have significant advantages in dealing with non-Euclidean data and have been widely used in various fields. However, most of the existing GNN models face two main challenges: (1) Most GNN models built upon the message-passing framework exhibit a shallow structure, which hampers their ability to efficiently transmit information between distant nodes. To address this, we aim to propose a novel message-passing framework, enabling the construction of GNN models with deep architectures akin to convolutional neural networks (CNNs), potentially comprising dozens or even hundreds of layers. (2) Existing models often approach the learning of edge and node features as separate tasks. To overcome this limitation, we aspire to develop a deep graph convolutional neural network learning framework capable of simultaneously acquiring edge embeddings and node embeddings. By utilizing the learned multi-dimensional edge feature matrix, we construct multi-channel filters to more effectively capture accurate node features.}
