\spara{Bias.} % of MLE for \bc}.
\label{sec:anl-est-bc}
In this section, we estimate the bias of the MLE, $\text{Bias}(\hat{\varepsilon}) = \mathbbm
E(\hat{\varepsilon}) - \varepsilon$.

First, we note that our case study can be connected to Rasch models~\cite{fischer200616}.
These models are used in item response theory to measure some latent traits of the personality from the outcomes of some questionnaires.
Rasch models assume that the probability that the person $j$ answer correctly to item $k$ depend on the ability of $j$, $\theta_j$ and on the item difficulty, $b_k$.
In particular, the probability of correct answer is  
\begin{align}
    P(u_{jk} = 1) = c_k + \frac{1 - c_k}{1 + \exp{(-A_k (\theta_j - b_k))}}.
\label{eq:rasch}
\end{align}
A higher person ability increases the probability of correct answer, while higher item difficulty decreases the probability of correct answer.
This probability is exactly the one that we are modeling in the sBCM, by simply associating the $\varepsilon$ to $\theta_j$ and $b_k$ to $\lvert x_i^t - x_j^t \rvert$.
A higher bounded confidence interval increases the probability of having a positive interaction, while a higher difference between agents' opinions decreases this probability.
Specifically, \Cref{eq:rasch} is equivalent to \Cref{eq:pijt} if $A_k = \rho$ \mynote[from=Jac]{$\rho$?}, $c_k = 0$, $\theta_j = \varepsilon$ for each $j$, and $b_k = \lvert x_i^t - x_j^t \rvert$.

Thus, we lead back the problem of estimating $\varepsilon$ observing $\order$ and $x^0$ to the estimation of $\theta$ in the Rasch model, while observing $b$.


