We make use of another graph parameter that is equivalent to bounding both~$\reduced{\omega}{G}$ and~$\cideg{G}$ simultaneously: the \emph{clique-degree}, denoted by~$\cdeg{G}$, which we call the maximum number of distinct maximal cliques that a maximal clique intersects, see \cref{fig:illustrate_different_parameters} for a comparison of these three parameters.

Perhaps the most powerful characterisation of these parameters being bounded is in terms of forbidden induced subgraphs. 
In \cref{sec:forbiddeninducedsubgraphs}, we see that forbidding a star (\cref{fig:matchingKI_and_matchingKK}(a)), a split matching (\cref{fig:matchingKI_and_matchingKK}(b)), a matching between two cliques (\cref{fig:matchingKI_and_matchingKK}(c)), an anti-matching between two cliques (\cref{fig:matchingKI_and_matchingKK}(d)), and a half-graph between two cliques (\cref{fig:matchingKI_and_matchingKK}(e)) as an induced subgraph is equivalent to being clique-sparse. 

