\subsubsection{Approximation accuracy} Table~\ref{tab:NumCollisions} presents the percentage of collisions observed in the sampled configuration data in both, the simulated and the real environment. As the separation distance between spheres decreases, the collision percentage for the fixed-sphere approximations gradually converges to that of the proposed methods. This occurs because the conservative approximation of the capsule using spheres necessitates an increase in sphere radius to ensure collision-free verification. In contrast, our approaches directly leverages the link structure, eliminating the need for such conservative approximations.
% Additionally, we observed that every configuration identified as a collision by our approach was also classified as a collision in the fixed-sphere approximation. Likewise, all configurations deemed collision-free using the fixed decomposition remained collision-free with our method.

\subsubsection{Performance} Table~\ref{tab:StatisticsCollCheck} shows the corresponding mean runtimes and number of collision queries per configuration. It can be seen, that both of our algorithms outperform fixed sphere decomposition of the manipulator in all considered resolutions. Our bi-directional approach yields an runtime decrease of approximately 80\% compared to the 10cm resolution and 40\% compared to the 50cm resolution.
