\begin{abstract}
Automated content-aware layout generation—the task of arranging visual elements such as text, logos, and underlays on a background canvas—remains a fundamental yet underexplored problem in intelligent design systems. While recent advances in deep generative models and large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in structured content generation, most existing approaches lack grounding in contextual design exemplars and fall short in handling semantic alignment and visual coherence. In this work, we introduce CAL-RAG, a Retrieval-Augmented, Agentic framework for content-aware layout generation that integrates multimodal retrieval, large language models, and collaborative agentic reasoning. Our system retrieves relevant layout examples from a structured knowledge base and invokes an LLM-based layout recommender to propose structured element placements. A vision-language grader agent evaluates the layout based on visual metrics, and a feedback agent provides targeted refinements, enabling iterative improvement. We implement our framework using LangGraph and evaluate on the PKU PosterLayout dataset, a benchmark rich in semantic and structural variability. CAL-RAG achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple layout metrics—including underlay effectiveness, element alignment, and overlap—substantially outperforming strong baselines such as LayoutPrompter. Our results demonstrate that combining retrieval augmentation with agentic multi-step reasoning provides a scalable, interpretable, and high-fidelity solution for automated layout generation.

%Content-aware layout generation, the task of automatically arranging visual elements on a canvas based on given content, is critical for various applications in graphic design such as poster and magazine creation. While numerous generative models have been proposed, many treat layout design as a numerical optimization problem, often overlooking the intricate semantic relationships inherent in design. Furthermore, the reliance on large, high-quality annotated datasets poses a significant challenge due to the scarcity and high cost of obtaining such data. Recent advancements have shown the potential of Large Language Models (LLMs) in generating structured outputs, including layouts, yet effectively harnessing their multi-modal capabilities for content-aware generation remains an open area of research. This paper introduces a novel approach that combines Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with an agentic framework for content-aware layout creation. The proposed model utilizes a RAG database, built upon the PKU PosterLayout dataset, to provide relevant layout examples to an LLM agent acting as a layout recommender. Subsequently, a VLM grader agent evaluates the generated layout based on predefined visual metrics criteria to determine its acceptability. If the layout is deemed unacceptable, a VLM feedback agent analyzes the shortcomings and provides specific feedback to the layout recommender for refinement. This multi-agent system leverages the PKU dataset, which defines element types like text, logo, and underlay, as a benchmark for training the RAG database and evaluating the model's performance using metrics such as underlay effectiveness, overlay, and alignment. The findings of this research demonstrate the potential of combining RAG with a collaborative multi-agent framework to advance the field of automated graphic design and the use of LLMs for complex creative tasks.
\end{abstract}

