\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

%\ml{need a more elaborate discussions here: give a quick summary, reiterate the take-away points, and finally mention possible future directions/open questions.}

In this work, 
% we introduced two abstraction-based approaches to synthesize controllers for nonlinear stochastic systems which reduce conservatism with respect to state-of-the-art approaches These rely on MI-MDPs and SMDPs, respectively. 
we introduced two abstraction-based approaches, namely MI-MDPs and SMDPs, for controller synthesis in nonlinear stochastic systems, both aimed at reducing the conservatism of existing methods.
% While the first one generalize existing abstractions, such as IMDPs, the second one is shown to be at least as tight, if not more, than any MI-MDP for the same discretization. 
MI-MDPs generalize prior abstractions such as IMDPs by allowing multiple, overlapping clusters, leading to tighter guarantees at the cost of increased memory and computation. In contrast, SMDPs are shown to be at least as tight as any MI-MDP under the same discretization, while offering significantly lower abstraction times and synthesis costs comparable to IMDPs.
% Although in MI-MDPs the decrease in conservatism is attained at the cost of higher memory consumption and computation times, SMDPs show smaller abstraction times than MI-MDPs and IMDPs, and exhibit synthesis times and memory consumption comparable to those of IMDPs. 
% We also provided a rich empirical evaluation that validates our theoretical results, and which also shows that SMDPs are able to mitigate the increase in conservatism caused by increasing the dimension of the system.
Our extensive empirical evaluation supports the theoretical findings and further demonstrates that SMDPs effectively mitigate the growth in conservatism with increasing system dimensionality.



Our ongoing research focuses on efficient data structures to reduce the computational complexity of SMDP abstractions. Another direction is the extension of SMDP abstractions to data-driven setting, where both vector field $f$ and disturbance distribution $P_W$ are unknown. 
% We highlight that, despite Theorem~\ref{thm:multi_imdp_vs_mdp_st} and Section~\ref{sec:case_studies} showing that SMDPs are tighter than MI-MDPs in the model-based setting, the latter might still be advantageous when the system's dynamics~\eqref{eq:sys} are uncertain, and the abstraction has to be constructed leveraging samples from trajectories of the system.
While Theorem~\ref{thm:multi_imdp_vs_mdp_st} and the case studies in Section~\ref{sec:case_studies} show that SMDPs yield tighter guarantees than MI-MDPs in model-based scenarios, MI-MDPs may offer advantages when the abstraction must be constructed from sampled system trajectories.


