\section{Introduction} 
\IEEEPARstart{E}lectric power grids are among the largest sources of greenhouse gas emissions, serving as crucial infrastructure to drive rapidly growing societal and industrial energy demands~\cite{poudyal2023resilience}. Notably, the electricity sector produces approximately 40\% of global carbon dioxide (CO$_2$) emissions~\cite{IEA2023}. Due to environmental and societal reasons, it is widely considered to transition from purely cost and reliability-driven grid operations towards sustainability-aware paradigms that explicitly account for the carbon emission impacts of generating electricity~\cite{olsen2018optimal}. For this reason, \emph{carbon accounting}, which traces electricity back to generation-related emissions, has gained increasing attention (as a basis for grid decarbonization planning and market design).~\cite{miller2022hourly,chen2024towards}. Hence, accurate carbon modeling for power systems lays the foundation for effective carbon evaluation, informed mitigation strategies, and the design of electricity markets and regulatory mechanisms.


Traditionally, power grid operations have focused mainly on cost minimization and reliability enhancements by employing optimization formulations such as economic dispatch or optimal power flow (OPF) under security constraints~\cite{cain2012history,ma2024opf} and linearized nonlinear power flow constraints~\cite{cho2023topology,cho2024data}.
%Traditionally, power grid operations have focused mainly on cost minimization and reliability enhancements by employing optimization formulations such as economic dispatch or optimal power flow (OPF) under security constraints~\cite{cain2012history,ma2024opf}.
Similarly, ongoing efforts to develop synthetic power grid test systems~\cite{Heidel2016GRIDDATA,birchfield2016statistical,birchfield2016grid} or to unify power network data formats~\cite{babaeinejadsarookolaee2019power} primarily focus on traditional metrics such as generation cost functions and network operational limits.
Thanks to a recent effort of the IEEE PES Task Force, a comprehensive standardized set of AC transmission system test cases has been developed, as termed by \texttt{PGLib-OPF}, the power grid library for OPF. This library includes the IEEE Power Flow test systems and synthetic large-scale test systems \cite{birchfield2016grid}, among other well-known systems, each with complete information on network topology, generation, and load data needed to formulate traditional OPF problems. This not only enables benchmarking various AC-OPF algorithms but also facilitates the generation of large-scale OPF-learning datasets, as in \cite{panciatici2024opfdata,joswig2022opf}.  
However, the \texttt{PGLib-OPF} test systems have not incorporated any carbon emission information for the generation resources, pointing out a critical gap for benchmarking carbon-aware computation and optimization solutions.


Driven by global sustainability concerns, there is a rapidly increasing R\&D effort to incorporate the impact of carbon emissions directly into power system computation and optimization tasks~\cite{li2023decarbonization,zhao2023emission,kang2012carbon}. 
Taking into account the emission level of CO$_2$, or CO$_2$-equivalent (CO$_2$e) that also includes methane and nitrous oxide, grid operators can better understand and evaluate both economic efficiency and environmental impacts~\cite{luo2023tradeoff, wang2024co2e}. Moreover, recent research has expanded traditional OPF formulations to incorporate carbon emissions costs and constraints directly, enabling a cleaner generation portfolio~\cite{chen2024carbon,sang2023encoding}.
In parallel, spatial load shifting that reallocates flexible demand across the grid are being explored to reduce system‐wide emissions~\cite{lindberg2022using,lindberg2021environmental,he2024long}. Despite these promising techniques, the lack of standardized test systems that directly include generator-side carbon information remains a critical barrier for benchmarking various R\&D work.
As a result, almost all research papers have to either come up with custom carbon settings or retrofit existing ones, possibly using an ad-hoc assignment. 
Although recent tools such as \texttt{ElectricityEmissions.jl} support system-wide and locational carbon emission metrics, they still do not provide generator-level carbon intensity assignments. Therefore, a unified carbon-enriched extension of power system test cases is still missing, yet essential for benchmarking the performance of  R\&D work in developing and validating carbon computing and optimization tasks.



Inspired by this need, we propose \texttt{PGLib-CO2}, which is built directly on the widely-adopted \texttt{PGLib-OPF} test systems and incorporates a standardized setting of CO$_2$ and CO$_2$e intensity factors to be consistent with the fuel‐type assignments therein. We extend the fuel categorization in \texttt{PGLib-OPF} to enable more precise generator carbon profiling. Our carbon‐enhancing tool is packaged for both Python’s \texttt{pandapower} and Julia’s \texttt{PowerModels.jl}, attaining a seamless integration of emission data into grid computation and optimization tasks. It also allows users to configure their own intensity factor values or develop general use cases. We demonstrate the utility of \texttt{PGLib-CO2} through case studies that quantify cost–emission trade-offs and optimize a carbon-aware objective function. The \texttt{PGLib-CO2} dataset is available at Github PGLib-CO2 repository\footnote{\url{https://github.com/jacobcho0103/PGLib-CO2}}.
In a nutshell, our main contributions are three-fold:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Standardized Carbon-Enriched Dataset:} \texttt{PGLib-CO2} provides open-source, user-friendly functions to incorporate carbon emission factors into \texttt{PGLib-OPF}  test systems in a systematic manner.
\item \textbf{Integration to Use Cases:} We demonstrate \texttt{PGLib-CO2}'s seamless integration with \texttt{pandapower} and  \texttt{PowerModels.jl} through case studies on carbon-aware optimization, quantifying cost–emission trade-offs.
\item \textbf{Reproducible, Open-source Research:} We deliver open-source codes and datasets for benchmarking carbon accounting and carbon-aware optimization, including developing new carbon emission metrics and optimization algorithms. 
\end{itemize}

The rest of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:Dataset} presents an overview of \texttt{PGLib-CO2} and the workflow to use it. Section~\ref{sec:usecase} discusses some key use cases of \texttt{PGLib-CO2}, highlighting the connection between emission modeling and standard computing and optimization tasks. Section~\ref{sec:sim} provides the experimental test results that utilize \texttt{PGLib-CO2} and its generated dataset. Concluding remarks and future research directions are offered in Section~\ref{sec:con}.

