\section{Overview of \texttt{PGLib-CO2}}
\label{sec:Dataset}
This section presents how \texttt{PGLib-CO2} performs generator-level carbon profiling to support carbon emissions modeling and calculations in power systems.
The key lies in associating the active power output of each generator with their carbon emission intensity factors.
%
As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{structure}, for bus $i$ connecting generation $g$ to the grid, we define $e^{\mathrm{G}}_g$ to denote the constant carbon emission intensity of generator $g$, expressed in tons per megawatt-hour (t/MWh). Letting $P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}$ (in MW) represent the active power output of any generator $g$ in the set $\ccalG$, we can compute its own carbon emissions as
\begin{equation}
    R^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} = e^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \cdot P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g},~~\forall g\in\ccalG.
    \label{eq:genEmission}
\end{equation}
If multiple generators are connected to bus $i$, their individual $R^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}$ values are aggregated to compute the total emissions at that bus. 
This generator-level emission modeling serves as a foundational step for downstream emission calculations and carbon-aware optimization tasks, as detailed later in Section \ref{sec:usecase}.
%\textcolor{blue}{(shall we talk more about how to determine eg here?)}


%Fuel type assignment
The intensity factor $e^{\mathrm{G}}_g$ is the key characteristic for carbon modeling of each generator $g$ and depends mainly on its fuel type~\cite{houghton1996climate}.
For example, clean energy sources such as wind, solar, and hydro have $e^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}=0$, while coal-fired units tend to have the highest $e^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}$ values. Based on this principle, \texttt{PGLib-CO2} has expanded the fuel type information originally provided in \texttt{PGLib-OPF}. 
The \texttt{PGLib-OPF} dataset includes only a few fuel types, such as distillate fuel oil (\textbf{PEL}), natural gas (\textbf{NG}), bituminous coal (\textbf{COW}), and nuclear (\textbf{NUC})), which are a subset of fuel types needed for precise carbon profiling~\cite{durvasulu2021data}.
To address this, our \texttt{PGLib-CO2} introduces a more detailed fuel classification that incorporates additional categories such as anthracite coal (\textbf{ANT}), combined-cycle gas turbines (\textbf{CCGT}), internal combustion engines (\textbf{ICE}), and the aforementioned clean energy sources.


%CO2 and CO2e
Following the research on quantifying carbon emissions, \texttt{PGLib-CO2} has also provided two types of intensity factors for each fuel type: One for quantifying the CO$_2$ emissions and the other for CO$_2$e emissions. The inclusion of CO$_2$e values is crucial as the latter accounts for the impact of methane (CH$_4$) and nitrous oxide (N$_2$O) in addition to CO$_2$, thereby offering a more complete assessment of global warming potentials \cite{houghton1996climate}. 

Table~\ref{tab:emission_factors} presents the complete list of fuel types and their corresponding intensity factors that have been adopted by \texttt{PGLib-CO2}. These fuel types and factors follow from well-established research \cite{houghton1996revised}. By providing both CO$_2$ and CO$_2$e metrics, the dataset offers researchers the flexibility to select the factor that best aligns with their specific analytical needs and ensures consistency with existing literature.




\subsection{Workflow for Using \texttt{PGLib-CO2}} 
\texttt{PGLib-CO2} has designed a streamlined workflow to enrich the standard \texttt{PGLib-OPF} dataset with the generator carbon characteristics. 
A detailed example of this process is available in the Appendix.
First, it starts with loading the network data from \texttt{PGLib-OPF} test systems into one of the two environments, either Python using \texttt{pandapower}~\cite{pandapower2018} or Julia using \texttt{PowerModels.jl}~\cite{powermodels2018}. Second, one can use the \texttt{fuel\_dict\_generation} function to create a dictionary that maps generators to their fuel types and assigns CO$_2$ as the default type of intensity factors.
Note that this dictionary is fully editable, allowing users to access and change values according to their specific system, such as reassigning a fuel type or switching the intensity factor to CO$_2$e.
%Note that users can manually customize both features.
Finally, this customized dictionary is passed to the \texttt{carbon\_casefile} function to embed the fuel type and intensity factor as formal attributes within the generator data structure. This step is necessary because fuel type information exists only within inaccessible comments in the original \texttt{PGLib-OPF} database.
Through the \texttt{PGLib-CO2} format conversion, the resultant test cases are suitable for direct use in carbon-aware analysis or optimization in either Python or Julia.



%%%%%%%%%%
%Integrating carbon attributes into traditional case file is achieved through a function-based approach that seamlessly augments existing PGLib test cases. Using dictionary-based data structures in Python’s \texttt{pandapower}~\cite{pandapower2018} and Julia’s \texttt{PowerModels.jl}~\cite{powermodels2018}, each generator is mapped to its corresponding fuel type and emission factor. Consequently, power flow and optimal power flow (OPF) analyses are naturally extended to include carbon emissions, facilitating the systematic exploration of environmental impacts under varying load profiles, fuel costs, and policy constraints. This enhanced framework provides a robust foundation for evaluating the interaction between economic operation and environmental performance, ultimately supporting more effective decarbonization strategies in large-scale power systems.






