\section{Use Cases of \texttt{PGLib-CO2}}
\label{sec:usecase}
This section presents the key use cases of \texttt{PGLib-CO2} that can extend classical power system computations and optimizations to account for carbon emissions. Through generator-level carbon profiling, \texttt{PGLib-CO2} enables a variety of emerging R\&D efforts in carbon-aware grid operations.
%To demonstrate the broad applicability of the \texttt{PGLib-CO2} tool, we present some of its key use cases by extending classical power system computation/optimization tasks to quantify and mitigate the impact of carbon emissions. 


\subsection{Computing Carbon Emission Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}
A fundamental use case enabled by \texttt{PGLib-CO2} is to compute and track carbon emissions produced by generation plants, by using both system-wide and location-based metrics.  This carbon computation task is the basis for evaluating the environmental impact of electricity generation. This capability directly supports \textbf{carbon accounting} practices where emissions are attributed to end users to clarify responsibility in planning and operational decisions of energy demands~\cite{chen2024towards}. It is actively pursued by datacenter operators~\cite{lindberg2021environmental, radovanovic2022carbon} and other large consumers~\cite{google2018}.
In particular, the computation of location-based carbon emissions remains a vibrant area of research aimed at ensuring fair attribution of emission responsibility across network nodes~\cite{lu2024market,gorka2024electricityemissionsjl}.


First and foremost, we define the \textbf{system-wide total emissions} obtained by summing over the emissions in \eqref{eq:genEmission} from all generators in ${\mathcal G}$, as
\begin{align}
    R^{\mathrm{tot}} = \sum_{g \in {\mathcal{G}}} R^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}
                     = \sum_{g \in {\mathcal{G}}} e^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \cdot P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}.
    \label{eq:totalEmission}
\end{align}
The total emissions $R^{\mathrm{tot}}$ can be used to compute several metrics, as listed here. 
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Average carbon emission (ACE):} Based on the total load demand $\sum_{i=1}^N P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$, the ACE metric is defined as
\begin{align}
    e^{\mathrm{ACE}}:=\frac{R^{\mathrm{tot}}}{\textstyle \sum_{i=1}^N P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}}.
\end{align}
ACE captures the average carbon footprint per unit of demand and is widely used to evaluate system-wide carbon efficiency~\cite{sotos2015amendment}.


\item \textbf{Locational marginal carbon emission (LMCE):} While the ACE metric is useful for understanding the system-wide performance, it does not provide the sensitivity of carbon emissions to locational demand changes. The LMCE metric can offer this spatial variability needed for carbon computation~\cite{ruiz2010analysis}. At bus $i$, it is defined for an incremental change in load $P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$ by
\begin{equation}
e^{\mathrm{LMCE}}_i := \frac{dR^{\mathrm{tot}}}{dP^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}}. %= \sum_{g \in \boldsymbol{\mathcal{G}}} e^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \cdot \frac{d P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}}{dP^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}}.
\end{equation}
Thus, LMCE quantifies the sensitivity of total system emissions based on the generation redispatch rules for serving an incremental load change at a specific bus. It can be analytically computed using the market optimization model through Lagrangian sensitivity analysis \cite{lu2024market}. We will adopt a numerical difference approach in the simulations by changing $P^{\mathrm{D}}_i$ by a small amount (e.g.,~1MW) and directly resolving the dispatch to find the change of $R^{\mathrm{tot}}$~\cite{he2021using}.

Although LMCE has the spatial variability, it can represent only \textit{marginal sensitivity} and is insufficient for analyzing larger load shifts or non-marginal generator redispatch~\cite{gorka2024electricityemissionsjl}.
However, its bus-based granularity makes LMCE a valuable signal for real-time grid operations under the spatial flexibility in load demands.



\item \textbf{Locational average carbon emissions (LACE):} There are ongoing R\&D efforts to design a new metric that can combine the strengths of ACE and LMCE to quantify location-specific average carbon impacts. One notable approach towards LACE is the network-based \textbf{carbon emission flow (CEF) model}~\cite{kang2012carbon,kang2015carbon}.
The CEF model treats power flows as fluid flows, and disaggregates the carbon emissions at any node to all outflow directions using the proportional power sharing principle.
Although this principle is mathematically sound, it does not follow the Kirchhoff’s circuit laws or the electricity market responses, and thus the CEF model can incur unfair carbon allocations during the disaggregation of power flows. For example, a recent paper \cite{wang2023carbon} has shown a paradox in CEF-based carbon allocation due to the presence of counter‐directional loop flows in meshed networks.
%Counter‐directional loop flows can cancel carbon emission flows under the proportional sharing principle. This causes the CEF model to misallocate emissions and fail to reflect the actual system operating conditions.}


To address this issue, the latest attempt is to incorporate the market-dispatch information along an integration path to compute the overall carbon emissions for serving a given load demand~\cite{lu2024market}. This dispatch-based LACE metric can well match the market dispatch, but it is highly sensitive to the chosen integration path and may fail to fully capture the global dispatch information for regions not on the path. 
Thus, definitions and computation methods for LACE are still an ongoing research direction. Thankfully, \texttt{PGLib-CO2} can conveniently integrate  different approaches for this metric.   
\end{enumerate}

In a nutshell, all these carbon emission metrics offer complementary perspectives for quantifying the impact of load demands, each with distinct advantages and limitations. The \texttt{PGLib-CO2} dataset has been designed to generalize both the existing approaches and future needs in carbon computation.

