\subsection{Carbon-aware Optimal Power Flow (OPF)}\label{sec:opf}
The OPF problem, a fundamental power system optimization task, can be extended to incorporate carbon-related objectives and constraints to form a carbon-aware OPF \cite{chen2024carbon,sang2023encoding}.
A basic approach is to add a carbon-associated cost to each generator based on a fixed carbon tax rate~\cite{olsen2018optimal}.
Let $c^{\mathrm{E}}_g(\cdot)$ and $c^{\mathrm{C}}_g(\cdot)$ denote the economic and carbon cost functions for generator $g\in\ccalG$. We can express the latter as 
\begin{align}
c^{\mathrm{C}}_g(P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}) = (\tau \cdot e^{\mathrm{G}}_g) \times P^{\mathrm{G}}_g,
\end{align}
with $\tau$ denoting the carbon tax rate in dollars per ton of emissions (\$/t).
Incorporating this cost into the conventional \textit{carbon-neutral} OPF objective yields:
\begin{subequations}\label{OPF}
\begin{align}
    \min ~ &\textstyle \sum_{g\in\ccalG} \left\{c^{\mathrm{E}}_{g} (P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}) + c^{\mathrm{C}}_g(P^{\mathrm{G}}_g) \right\}\label{OPF_obj}\\
    \textrm{s.t.} ~
    &\underline{V}_i \leq V_{i} \leq \bar{V}_i,~ \underline{\theta}_{ij} \leq \theta_{ij} \leq \bar{\theta}_{ij},\label{OPF1}\\
    &\underline{P}^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \leq P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \leq \bar{P}^{\mathrm{G}}_{g},~\underline{Q}^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \leq Q^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} \leq \bar{Q}^{\mathrm{G}}_{g},\label{OPF2}\\
    &\underline{P}_{ij} \leq P_{ij} \leq \bar{P}_{ij}, ~ P^2_{ij}+Q^2_{ij} \leq (\bar{S}_{ij})^2,\label{OPF3}\\
    & P_{ij} = V_{i}V_{j}(G_{ij}\cos \theta_{ij} +B_{ij}\sin \theta_{ij}),\label{OPF4}\\
    & Q_{ij} = V_{i}V_{j}(G_{ij}\sin \theta_{ij} -B_{ij}\cos \theta_{ij}),\label{OPF5}\\
    & \textstyle \sum_{g\in\ccalG_i}  P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} - P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i} = \textstyle \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{L} } P_{ij},\label{OPF6}\\
    & \textstyle \sum_{g\in\ccalG_i}  Q^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} - Q^{\mathrm{D}}_{i} = \textstyle \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{L} } Q_{ij},\label{OPF7}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
where the series admittance value for any branch $(i,j)\in\ccalL$ is given by $(G_{ij}+\upj B_{ij})$. 
The operational limits and power flow constraints in \eqref{OPF1}–\eqref{OPF7} follow from the \textit{carbon-neutral} OPF problem.
This formulation in \eqref{OPF} demonstrates a direct application of \texttt{PGLib-CO2} to account for environmental impacts along with considerations of economic cost.
Of course, generalizing \eqref{OPF} to include location-specific or temporal carbon constraints (for multi-period OPF models), is also possible but could be challenging to solve; see e.g., the recent work~\cite{chen2024carbon,sang2023encoding}. For simplicity, we will solve \eqref{OPF} in the simulations to demonstrate the effects of using a carbon-sensitive objective only. 




