\subsection{Carbon-aware Optimal Load Shifting (OLS)}\label{sec:ols}
Beyond generation adjustments, load-side flexibility offers further opportunities to reduce carbon emissions, which can be effectively modeled by \texttt{PGLib-CO2}. We highlight this possibility by providing an example of the carbon-aware OLS problem~\cite{lindberg2021environmental}.
%
We consider it as an extension of OPF in \eqref{OPF} by allowing for load flexibility to replace the fixed load demand $P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$ with an adjustable value $\hat{P}^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$. This change essentially expands the feasible region and thus can further reduce the objective cost value in \eqref{OPF_obj}.
%
With the spatial variability discussed in Section \ref{sec:metrics}, OLS works by reallocating the demand from high-emission to low-emission locations, thereby reducing total emissions without curtailing the overall energy consumption.
%
Adding the load flexibility into the carbon-aware OPF yields:
\begin{subequations}\label{ls}
\begin{align}
    \min ~ &\textstyle \sum_{g\in\ccalG} \left\{c^{\mathrm{E}}_{g} (P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g}) + c^{\mathrm{C}}_g(P^{\mathrm{G}}_g) \right\}\label{OLS_obj}\\
    \textrm{s.t.} ~
    &\eqref{OPF1}-\eqref{OPF5}~\text{and}~\eqref{OPF7},\\
    & \textstyle \sum_{g\in\ccalG_i}  P^{\mathrm{G}}_{g} - \hat{P}^{\mathrm{D}}_{i} = \textstyle \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{L}} P_{ij}, \label{ls_balance}\\
    & (1-\epsilon) P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i} \leq \hat{P}^{\mathrm{D}}_{i} \leq (1+\epsilon) P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}, \label{ls_range}\\
    & \textstyle \sum_{i=1}^{N} \hat{P}^{\mathrm{D}}_{i} = \textstyle \sum_{i=1}^{N} P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}. \label{ls_total}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
%Note that the OLS problem includes a new decision variable $\hat{P}^\mathrm{D}_{i}$ for the adjustable load demand per bus $i$, as used by the updated nodal balance in \eqref{ls_balance}. 
Note that the OLS problem includes a new decision variable $\hat{P}^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$ for the updated nodal balance in \eqref{ls_balance}. 
The constraints in \eqref{ls_range} bound each $\hat{P}^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$ to be within a given threshold level $\epsilon$ of its nominal value $P^{\mathrm{D}}_{i}$. More importantly, the equality in \eqref{ls_total} ensures that the total system demand remains constant, allowing emission reductions to be achieved through load reallocation rather than demand reduction.
Using this targeted shifting of demand, OLS can further improve the utilization of low-carbon generation resources over OPF, without compromising overall demand consumption or operational feasibility.
Like the OPF problem, setting the carbon cost $c^{\mathrm{C}}_g(\cdot)=0$ in \eqref{OLS_obj} leads to \textit{carbon-neutral} OLS that is related to optimal demand response problem~\cite{hayes2014optimal}. %This will be termed as carbon-neutral OLS later on. 
Note that this OLS formulation in \eqref{ls} requires the operator to precisely know the amount of load shifting capabilities given in \eqref{ls_range}-\eqref{ls_total}, which could be restrictive in practical markets. More realistic OLS formulations have been developed in \cite{lindberg2022using,he2024long}, but for simplicity, we will use \eqref{ls} in the numerical studies. 


