\section{Numerical Results}\label{sec:sim}

We demonstrate the capabilities of \texttt{PGLib-CO2} through numerical evaluations of the carbon metrics and carbon-aware optimization using the IEEE 30-bus  and 118-bus test systems. We have used the network data from \texttt{pglib\_opf\_case30\_ieee} and \texttt{pglib\_opf\_case118\_ieee} in \texttt{PGLib-OPF}. To better demonstrate the impact of carbon-aware solutions, we have slightly modified the generator fuel types given by \texttt{PGLib-OPF}, which tend to have very similar emission intensities and lead to small spatial variability. Specifically, we have reconfigured the systems by assigning each generator to one of three fuel types: high-emission anthracite coal (ANT), low-emission gas combined cycle (CCGT), or zero-emission renewables. This modification has effectively increased the variability of the system-wide emission profile, making the impact of carbon-aware methods more pronounced. We have used the corresponding CO$_2$e factors for these three fuel types given in Table~\ref{tab:emission_factors}, with the specific assignment listed in Table~\ref{tab:fuel_sim}. For the load profiles, we have used the nominal demands specified in the case files to evaluate both OPF and OLS performance. All optimization problems have been formulated in Julia using JuMP~\cite{lubin2023jump} with PowerModels.jl~\cite{powermodels2018} and solved using the IPOPT 3.14.4 optimizer~\cite{wachter2006implementation} on a laptop with an Intel\textsuperscript{\textregistered} CPU @ 2.70 GHz and 32 GB of RAM.







