\subsection{Carbon-aware OPF Simulation Results}
%I try to follow the description style in Data-driven paper
We validate the effectiveness of carbon-aware OPF by comparing its operational cost and carbon emissions with carbon-neutral OPF.
The operational cost is referred to as the total generator economic cost $\sum_{g\in\ccalG}c^E_g(P^G_g)$ in \eqref{OPF_obj}, while the carbon emissions are evaluated by $R^{\mathrm{tot}}$.
To highlight the impact of using the carbon-sensitive objective in \eqref{OPF_obj}, we use the results of carbon-neutral OPF as the baseline values to normalize the carbon-aware OPF outputs.
Table~\ref{tab:opf_results} lists the resulting normalized costs versus emissions for the two test systems. Note that for this table and the following table on OLS results, the \textbf{bold} font is used to indicate the best performance in each metric. We consider the carbon tax rates $\tau$ of \$10/t, \$20/t, and \$30/t.
%For the 30-bus system, the carbon-neutral OPF yields the lowest operational cost but produces more than 200 tonnes of carbon emissions.
For the 30-bus system, using a carbon tax of \$10/t, the carbon-aware OPF reduces the emissions by 38.4\% compared to the carbon-neutral OPF. This reduction is achieved by increasing the operational costs by 17.4\%.
Further increases in the tax rate continue to reduce emissions at the expense of modestly higher operational costs.
This shows the trade-off when steering the dispatch toward cleaner units. Similar trade-offs are consistent across different systems. For the 118-bus system, a carbon tax of \$30/t leads to a 37.3\% emission reduction with a 15.9\% increase in operational cost.
In short, we have shown that carbon-aware OPF can effectively reshape generation portfolios toward lower-emitting resources, providing notable environmental benefits with manageable economic impact.






