\subsection{Carbon-aware OLS Simulation Results}
We further validate carbon-aware OLS with a load flexibility of $\epsilon=0.3$.
Table~\ref{tab:ols_results} lists the same two metrics by using the carbon-neutral OPF solution as the normalization baseline again. Because OLS is a special extension of OPF to include load flexibility, using the same baseline can better demonstrate the additional benefits of the extension.
It also ensures that any changes from the OPF results arise solely from the expansion of the feasible region enabled by load shifting.
%conventional OLS vs conventional OPF (baseline)
For the 30-bus system, the carbon-neutral OLS results in a 0.4\% decrease in operational cost relative to the carbon-neutral OPF baseline, with no significant change in emissions.
%carbon-aware OLS vs conventional OPF (baseline)
In contrast, the carbon-aware OLS with a \$10/t carbon tax has reduced emissions by 38.7\%, with an increase in operational costs by 17.1\%.
%carbon-aware OLS vs carbon-aware OPF for identifying additional efforts on the load-side
More importantly, when compared to the carbon‐aware OPF counterparts in Table~\ref{tab:opf_results}, carbon-aware OLS has reduced both operational costs and carbon emissions. This comparison confirms that load-side flexibility can provide an additional optimization dimension, further enhancing the improvements achievable through generation-side adjustments alone.


The advantages of carbon-aware OLS become more pronounced in the larger 118-bus system, showing better carbon reduction potentials.
In this system, carbon-neutral OLS reduces operational costs by 1.5\% while increasing the total emissions by 0.9\% over the carbon-neutral OPF baseline. Optimizing solely the economic cost can shift demand to cheaper yet more carbon-intensive generators, potentially increasing system-wide emissions to serve the same total load demand.
Carbon-aware OLS reverses this trend: for every $\tau$, it delivers substantial emission reductions relative to the carbon-neutral OPF baseline. Furthermore, the former outperforms carbon-aware OPF in both cost reduction and carbon mitigation. For example, carbon-aware OLS with a carbon tax of \$30/t achieves an additional 3.7\% emissions reduction and a 0.9\% lower operating cost compared to carbon-aware OPF.
These results underscore that coupling carbon pricing with demand-side flexibility can unlock deeper decarbonization potentials and avoid the perverse outcomes of cost-only, carbon-neutral OLS.





