\section{Conclusions and Future Work}\label{sec:con}
We developed the \texttt{PGLib-CO2} dataset, a novel extension of the widely used \texttt{PGLib-OPF} that incorporates carbon attributes of generator fuel types and intensity factors.
\texttt{PGLib-CO2} enriches standard network case files with CO$_2$ and CO$_2$-equivalent emission intensity factors assigned by the extended fuel categorization in \texttt{PGLib-OPF}.
\texttt{PGLib-CO2} is packaged for both Python’s \texttt{pandapower} and Julia’s \texttt{PowerModels.jl}, attaining a seamless integration of emission data into grid computation and optimization tasks. Users can immediately implement the default intensity values or customize them with their own values, supporting flexible analyses across a variety of scenarios. We demonstrated its effectiveness through carbon‑aware optimal power flow (OPF) and optimal load shifting (OLS) case studies using the standard IEEE test systems, highlighting how \texttt{PGLib-CO2} reveals the cost–emissions trade‑offs and optimizes a carbon-aware objective function. By standardizing carbon-enhanced test cases and workflows, \texttt{PGLib-CO2} establishes an open-source, reproducible foundation for benchmarking carbon accounting and carbon-aware optimization.

Future expansions of \texttt{PGLib-CO2} include the addition of more complex test systems, comprehensive modeling of renewables and storage, and refined emission data calibrated by real-world conditions. Researchers may explore advanced sampling approaches or historical load/generation traces to create scenarios reflecting actual or extreme operational states. By offering a robust foundation for carbon-aware decision, \texttt{PGLib-CO2} paves the way for innovative work in addressing the carbon footprint of the electric energy infrastructure.



