\subsection{Comparison of Fractional-Order and Integer-Order Systems}
In this section, we demonstrate the advantages of using fractional-order systems for modeling non-Markovian processes. To generate non-Markovian signals, we use the systems defined in~\eqref{eq:logistic_map}-\eqref{eq:lotka-volterra} to create the necessary datasets. Our proposed algorithms are then applied to identify corresponding fractional-order systems. Additionally, we employ a data-driven system identification algorithm for integer-order systems introduced in~\cite{narayanan2020disentangling}, using the same datasets.

To evaluate the impact of system order on both the system response and memory dependent behavior, we compare the responses of the inferred fractional-order and integer-order systems. The system trajectories obtained from the identified models are illustrated in Figures~\ref{fig:vanderpol_fractional_system_response_1}-\ref{fig:capacitor_fractional_system_response_1}. These simulations clearly show that integer-order models fail to accurately capture the dynamics of non-Markovian systems. Moreover, the approximation error increases as the fractional order~$\alpha$ decreases, reflecting stronger memory dependence—consistent with observations in~\cite{gupta2021non, flandrin1992wavelet}.


